How it works:
- The parser sets `importWrapper=true` on function messages tagged with `@importWrapper`. `addFileImports` in `std://file-imports.convo` now has that tag.
- When the LLM calls an import wrapper function, the call is parsed but not appended, and the function executes normally. After that, `appendImportWrapper` writes the messages:
  - `startFileImport` carries the prefix tags (such as `@time`) and `@toolId`.
  - `endFileImport` carries the `@import {path} !file` tags and no `@time`.
  - Both messages get the source path from `__file`, or from the last message with a source path.
- During flattening, `startFileImport` is skipped and `endFileImport` becomes a flat `function` message:
  - `called` is the wrapper function.
  - `calledParams` is `{paths:[...]}`, using the function's first param label.
  - `calledReturn` is the paths array.
  - Tags from the start message are merged in, so the `toolId` matches.

  This mirrors how a normal call and result pair flattens into a single message.
- Auto-complete continues after the wrapper, so the imported content is sent back to the LLM.
- Setting `__disableImportWrappers` to true falls back to the standard `call` and `result` messages. A call written manually as the last message also uses the standard flow.

A few limitations:
- In wrapper mode, only `__resultImports` is written. Any other shared variables set by a wrapper function are not persisted, because `endFileImport` has no body.
- `startFileImport` has no function name, matching your example. If more than one `@importWrapper` function is defined, the first one is used. A function name can optionally be given in the head, for example `> startFileImport addFileImports`, to choose a specific one.


